{"numberOfHits":1,"results":[{"id":"GO:0036166","isObsolete":false,"name":"phenotypic switching","definition":{"text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. Phenotypic switching involves changes in cell morphology and altered gene expression patterns. For example, Candida albicans switches from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues.","xrefs":[{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"12443899"},{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"22406749"},{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"8456504"},{"dbCode":"Wikipedia","dbId":"Phenotypic_switching"}]},"comment":"Mating type switching is not considered a type of phenotypic switching: for mating type switching, consider instead annotating to 'mating type switching ; GO:0007533'.","synonyms":[{"name":"phenotypic dimorphism","type":"related"}],"replaces":[{"id":"GO:1900447","type":"consider"},{"id":"GO:0036167","type":"replaced_by"}],"children":[{"id":"GO:1900240","relation":"negatively_regulates"},{"id":"GO:1900239","relation":"regulates"},{"id":"GO:0090677","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0044663","relation":"part_of"},{"id":"GO:1900241","relation":"positively_regulates"}],"history":[{"timestamp":"2016-05-06","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. Phenotypic switching begins with changes in cell morphology and altered gene expression patterns and ends when the morphology of a population of cells has reverted back to the default state, accompanied by altered expression patterns. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2016-05-06","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. Phenotypic switching involves changes in cell morphology and altered gene expression patterns. For example, Candida albicans switches from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-12-23","action":"Added","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0009987 (cellular process)"},{"timestamp":"2012-12-23","action":"Deleted","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0044699 (single-organism process)"},{"timestamp":"2012-10-19","action":"Added","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0044699 (single-organism process)"},{"timestamp":"2012-10-19","action":"Deleted","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0008150 (biological_process)"},{"timestamp":"2012-08-10","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. Phenotypic switching begins with changes in cell morphology and altered gene expression patterns and ends when the morphology of a population of cells has reverted back to the default state, accompanied by altered expression patterns. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-08-10","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-05-20","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one phenotype to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-05-20","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one cell type or form to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-03-29","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A reversible switch of a cell from one phenotype to another, at a frequency above the expected frequency for somatic mutations. For example, Candida albicans switches from a unicellular form to an invasive multicellular filamentous form upon infection of host tissue, and from white cells to opaque cells for sexual mating. Phenotypic switching also occurs in multicellular organisms; smooth muscle cells (SMCs) exhibit phenotypic transitions to allow rapid adaption to fluctuating environmental cues."},{"timestamp":"2012-03-29","action":"Added","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0008150 (biological_process)"},{"timestamp":"2012-03-29","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"phenotypic dimorphism"},{"timestamp":"2012-03-29","action":"Added","category":"TERM","text":"phenotypic switching"}],"aspect":"biological_process","usage":"Unrestricted"}],"pageInfo":null}